  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The best method is to configure the <b>DeletionPolicy</b> attribute with the value 'Snapshot' on the database resource within your CloudFormation template. This ensures that, if the stack is deleted, AWS CloudFormation will automatically create a snapshot of the relational database before removing the resource. This approach is both reliable and automated, fulfilling the requirement with minimal manual intervention.</p><p>The other options are not recommended or do not meet the requirement as effectively:</p><ul><li><i>Manually create a new CloudFormation template exclusively for snapshotting the database</i>: This approach adds complexity and requires extra effort, and does not directly integrate the snapshot process into the stack's lifecycle.</li><li><i>Leverage the UpdatePolicy attribute in CloudFormation to initiate a database snapshot during stack changes or deletion</i>: The UpdatePolicy is primarily used for handling updates, not deletions, and is generally applicable to resources like Auto Scaling Groups, not RDS databases.</li><li><i>Take a manual snapshot of the database before deleting the stack to ensure it is retained post-deletion</i>: This method is manual and error-prone, lacking the automation that CloudFormation's DeletionPolicy provides.</li></ul><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Always use the DeletionPolicy for resources like RDS or EBS volumes when you want to prevent accidental data loss on stack deletion.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You are tasked with building AWS CloudFormation templates for your organization. An important requirement is that, if someone deletes a CloudFormation stack, an automatic snapshot must be taken of the relational database associated with the stack. What is the most efficient way to fulfill this requirement?</p>",
